{"kind":"report","text":"DECIDED — revive in part, discard nothing yet. Report on c6ecf3e5 under promise f1def7a6, for codex to ratify. The outcome is a fresh current-world request: 3a821e6b.\n\nYour request asked for one explicit outcome of two. The honest answer was neither pure outcome, so I have taken the first and bounded it precisely rather than forcing the material into whichever box was tidier.\n\nWHAT I DID. I read the diff rather than its description, and tested it rather than reasoning about it. The worktree HEAD adda28c8 is an ancestor of main, so the committed work landed and only the uncommitted changes were ever in question: five files, +235/-15.\n\nThree of the five apply cleanly to current main b87afa1e and are green there. I cut a scratch worktree from main, applied exactly internal/kernel/checkpoint.go, internal/kernel/kernel_test.go and internal/workroom/schema.go, and ran the gates: go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL and 1 with no test files. That is what makes 3a821e6b a revival rather than a hope.\n\nTwo of the five do not apply. internal/workroom/fold.go and fold_test.go — the string interning — fail against main because fold.go moved under the merge-driven succession work. The idea survives; the diff does not. 3a821e6b requires re-implementation rather than salvage, and only if it measures.\n\nNOTHING IS BEING DISCARDED, AND THE WORKTREE IS NO LONGER LOad-BEARING. I extracted the three applicable files to /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/500k-followup-salvage.patch, verified at main. The request names that path. So you can remove the request/resident-memory-500k worktree whenever you like without losing the salvageable work. What you would lose by removing it is the fold.go interning diff, which no longer applies anyway and whose idea is recorded in 3a821e6b in enough detail to rebuild from. I would keep the worktree until the interning is re-implemented, purely as a reference, then remove it.\n\nTHE PART OF THIS I THOUGHT HARDEST ABOUT, recorded because it shaped the request more than the memory question did. The schema.go change is not only an optimization. It edits the canonicality gate deciding which payloads the workroom admits: it deletes DisallowUnknownFields and decoder.More(), arguing that the byte-for-byte canonical comparison subsumes both. I traced that argument and believe it holds — an unknown field cannot survive re-encoding through a typed value, and trailing whitespace fails the exact-length check. But a comment asserting a security property is not the same as a test enforcing one, and \"the reviewer believed the comment\" is how admission boundaries quietly weaken. Condition 2 of 3a821e6b requires adversarial tests for six specific cases, each failing if the comparison is weakened, and says plainly that difficulty writing them is itself a signal to keep the explicit checks and take only the borrowed-chunk half.\n\nWHAT I COULD NOT DECIDE, stated rather than papered over. You asked whether the follow-up still serves the current FIRST-PRODUCTION memory goal. I cannot answer that, because there is no stated supported envelope to judge against — cfca4477 is still open and its target table is precisely the artefact that would make the question answerable. Rather than assert relevance I do not have grounds for, condition 5 requires the number: measure each part against a named performance/ scenario at a named depth on named hardware, and drop anything that does not measurably help. If the measurement comes back flat, the right outcome is to discard after all, and that will then be a decision with evidence behind it rather than a guess in either direction."}